{"context":"The workspace registry (ADR-014) identifies a repository by **normalized absolute path**: `RegisterRepo` derives the repository name from the path basename, `FindRepo(abs)` resolves by exact path match, and the resolution gate is exact-path (\"the command must run inside the repository root\", ADR-013). The repository's default namespace is **derived dynamically** by the sync push (existing snapshot header → most common namespace → error).\n\nThree real problems follow:\n\n1. **Git worktrees and clones at different paths are invisible.** A worktree (or a second clone) of the same repository is a different absolute path → `FindRepo` misses → \"repository is not registered\" (exit 1), even though the knowledge is the same platform of the same project. Worktree-based item workflows (one worktree per item/platform) are therefore broken today.\n2. **Rename = identity change.** The repository name is the path basename; renaming the directory changes `source_repo` provenance and detaches the history.\n3. **Auto-registration guesses wrong.** `eka sync` auto-registers with project = basename; three platform repositories (api/web/mobile) become three separate projects unless every user remembers the explicit `--name` flag — and the namespace default is a fragile dynamic inference, not a stable fact.\n\nThe platform-scoped namespace is load-bearing: it is part of the unit identity (`form = ns/type:id:v`, the global primary key of `object_refs`). A namespace that can silently change invalidates resolution and provenance.","decision":"Every EKA repository carries a small, versioned metadata file at its root — `eka.yaml` — that records its **identity**: the project it belongs to, its stable repository name, and its canonical namespace. The pair **(project, namespace) is the immutable identity** of the repository; the path becomes an auxiliary.\n\n1. **`eka.yaml` schema (v1)** — generated by `eka init`, versioned in git, edited freely **before first sync**:\n\n   ```yaml\n   version: 1\n   project: atrium        # project id — the project grouping (was: --name flag)\n   name: api              # stable repository name — replaces the path basename\n   namespace: atrium-api  # canonical namespace — platform scope, immutable\n   ```\n\n   All three identifiers follow the existing valid-namespace rule: lowercase letters, digits, hyphens. `name` must not equal the reserved `runtime` (ADR-014 provenance sentinel).\n\n2. **Resolution is identity-based, path is auxiliary.** `FindRepo` first searches upward from the current directory for `eka.yaml`; when found, resolution is the store lookup by `(project_id, name)` — the path no longer needs to match. Git worktrees, subdirectories and renames resolve to the same registered repository. Repositories without `eka.yaml` keep the legacy exact-path resolution (backward compatible).\n\n3. **Registration reads the metadata.** `eka sync` (auto-registration) and `eka project register` inside a repository with `eka.yaml` register `(project, name, namespace)` from the file — never from the basename — and write `repos.namespace` immediately (no more dynamic most-common inference for metadata-carrying repositories). A metadata/registry mismatch (e.g. `project` in the file differs from the registered project) is a **deterministic error with a fix hint** — never a silent override, never auto-resolve.\n\n4. **`source_repo` provenance = metadata `name`.** The stable name replaces the path basename as `source_repo` in the canonical store, so renames and worktrees never fragment provenance. This is a **breaking change for existing stores** (the provenance pair changes) — the migration is documented in the spec; existing workspace stores must re-sync or be rebuilt.\n\n5. **Immutability.** After the first sync of a metadata-carrying repository, editing `project`, `name` or `namespace` in `eka.yaml` is refused with a deterministic error (the identity pair is frozen; changing it would change every unit identity and detach history). Renaming the project/namespace is an explicit future migration feature — deliberately **not** provided in this milestone.\n\n6. **Flags.** `--project` is removed from the repo-scoped authoring commands (`eka new`, `eka publish`); the project comes from the metadata. `--namespace` is removed from `eka new`: writing an artifact whose namespace differs from `repos.namespace` is a **refusal** (a repository is one platform — cross-platform access is a *read* via the qualified target `\u003cns\u003e/type:id`, which keeps working). Workspace-scoped commands (`status`, `project register/list`, `import`) are unaffected.\n\n7. **Worktree/duplicate conflict.** Two worktrees of the same repository syncing different content for the same identity is the existing overwrite path: the store is immutable, references are last-sync-wins, and the pull report's `Overwrites` list surfaces the replaced identities (existing mechanism, ADR-011/ADR-010) — no content auto-merge is introduced.","consequences":"- **Worktree item workflows work**: any worktree resolves to its repository identity; sync auto-registers once, then every worktree resolves.\n- **One identity, many paths**: rename the directory, move the clone, add worktrees — provenance and registration stay stable.\n- **Platform isolation for agents**: the (project, namespace) pair gives a deterministic per-platform identity; a container working items per platform resolves and syncs unambiguously — the platform filter feature (`eka get`/`eka view` per namespace) remains a separate additive feature (ADR-015 future-work), unaffected by this decision.\n- **Deterministic namespace**: metadata replaces dynamic inference for new repositories; legacy repositories keep the existing inference until they adopt `eka.yaml`.\n- **Breaking**: existing workspace stores see changed `source_repo` values after migration to metadata; the reference project gains an `eka.yaml` as the end-to-end proof; CLI docs and the runtime architecture documentation are updated.\n- **Rejected alternatives**: a UUID identity (unnecessary — (project, name) is already unique and human-readable); path-only resolution (rejected — the worktree problem is unsolvable without an identity carried by the repository); namespace inference forever (rejected — a load-bearing identity must not be dynamic); moving the registry into the repository (rejected — the store stays canonical per device; `eka.yaml` is identity, the store is runtime state — two layers with a documented boundary).\n\n### Compatibility \u0026 migration\n\n- Repositories without `eka.yaml` behave exactly as today (path-based resolution, basename registration, dynamic namespace).\n- Repositories with `eka.yaml`: first `eka sync` after adopting the file registers the metadata identity. If the repository was previously registered under a different project/name, the registry **refuses** with a fix hint (re-register explicitly or align the file) — no silent takeover.\n- Existing stores: `source_repo` values change for migrated repositories; the reference project snapshot is regenerated as part of the implementation.\n- Implementation checklist and file-by-file details: `reference/spec-repository-identity.md`.","alternativesConsidered":"- **UUID identity** — unnecessary: `(project, name)` is already unique and human-readable; a UUID adds indirection without solving a real problem.\n- **Path-only resolution (status quo)** — rejected: the worktree problem is unsolvable without an identity carried by the repository itself; every clone/worktree would need manual registration.\n- **Namespace inference forever** — rejected: a load-bearing identity (namespace is part of every unit identity) must not be a dynamic \"most common namespace\" inference that can silently change.\n- **Registry moved into the repository** — rejected: the store stays canonical per device; `eka.yaml` is identity (portable), the store is runtime state — two layers with a documented boundary.\n- **`--project` kept as an override** — rejected: an override that can differ from the immutable identity is a source of ambiguity; the internal query slot keeps the door open for future cross-project integration without a CLI flag today."}